## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design governance for cross-team experimentation, balancing speed with reliability and data integrity.

## Key Concepts
- KPI ownership and charter
- Preflight power analysis and data-quality gates
- Guardrails: latency, error rate, quotas
- Feature flags, canary and cohort rollouts
- Artifacts: experiment charter, data map, rollback plan

## Code Example
```javascript
function shouldRollout(experiment, metrics) {
  const {power, mde, dataQuality} = metrics;
  if (!experiment.flagsEnabled) return false;
  if (power < experiment.requiredPower) return false;
  if (!dataQuality.ok) return false;
  if (metrics.latency > experiment.maxLatency) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would thresholds vary by product area?
- How would you measure guardrail impact on velocity and reliability?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to design governance that aligns regulatory requirements with product velocity in a distributed, multi-region setup.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-domain decision rights and escalation paths
- SLOs/SLIs, error budgets, runbooks, postmortems
- Phased releases: canary, shadow traffic, regional residency checks
- Rollback strategies and incident playbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-gating check for progress
function canProceed(region, changes) {
  if (!regsVerified(region)) return false;
  if (latencyImpact(changes) > SLO.latency) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate regulatory verification across regions?
- How would you handle conflicting gate criteria between regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to translate reliability into a product mindset across multiple teams, define measurable outcomes, and design practical experiments that balance speed and quality.

## Key Concepts

- Observability as a product
- SLOs and error budgets
- Instrumentation with OpenTelemetry
- Incident rituals and blameless postmortems
- Toil reduction experiments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal OpenTelemetry trace init (illustrative)
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
const t = trace.getTracer('service');
const span = t.startSpan('request');
// business logic
span.end();
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this program across eight squads? 
- Which metrics would you track beyond MTTR and velocity?
